ucted numerous significant naval operations and achieved major victories for France in the western Mediterranean Sea, including the Battle of Cartagena in 1643."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-armand-de-maillé-brézé", "sourceName": "Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "battle-of-cartagena-1643", "targetName": "Battle of Cartagena (1643)", "context": "Maillé-Brézé's strategic command led to a decisive French victory over the Spanish fleet at the Battle of Cartagena on September 3, 1643."}, {"sourceSlug": "jean-armand-de-maillé-brézé", "sourceName": "Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "thirty-years-war", "targetName": "Thirty Years' War", "context": "Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé's entire naval career, marked by his rise to Grand Admiral and his major victories, occurred within the broader context of the Thirty Years' War, which ended two years after his death."}], "places": [{"name": "La Flèche, France", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Mediterranean Sea", "role": "Primary Theater of Operations"}, {"name": "Orbetello, Italy", "role": "Location of Death"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jean-armand-de-maillé-brézé"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2629600", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Armand_de_Maill%C3%A9,_2nd_Marquis_of_Br%C3%A9z%C3%A9"], "tags": ["France", "Political Leaders", "Europe", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé was a key figure in the rise of the French Royal Navy, achieving significant victories against Spain that shifted the balance of maritime power in the Mediterranean during the mid-17th century. His short but impactful career contributed to France's growing continental influence and set precedents for future naval strategy.", "significanceCategory": "continental"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-14T11:09:40Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43588491781", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jean Armand de Maillé, 2nd Marquis of Brézé, Duke of Fronsac was a French admiral.", "newValue": "Jean Armand de Maillé-Brézé (1619–1646), 2nd Marquis of Brézé and Duke of Fronsac, emerged as one of France's most formidable naval commanders during the tumultuous mid-17th century. A nephew of the all-powerful Cardinal Richelieu, France's chief minister, Maillé-Brézé's rapid ascent marked a pivotal period in the professionalization and expansion of the French Royal Navy under the Bourbon monarchy. His short but brilliant career, cut short at the Battle of Orbetello, cemented France's growing maritime power and challenged Spanish naval supremacy in the Mediterranean and Atlantic during the Franco-Spanish War, a critical theater of the wider Thirty Years' War.

Promoted to Grand Admiral of France at just 24 in 1640, Maillé-Brézé commanded significant French fleets, achieving decisive victories that reshaped the naval balance of power. His notable triumphs included the Battle of Barcelona in 1642, where he successfully lifted a Spanish siege, and the Battle of Cartagena in 1643, a strategic engagement off the Spanish coast that inflicted heavy losses on the Spanish fleet. These victories were crucial in securing French control over key coastal areas and disrupting Spanish lines of communication, directly contributing to France's ascendancy in the ongoing European conflicts.

